 "does_it_predict": {
  "_question": "Whether pace is worth WIRING, as opposed to whether it is measurable. Handing over a number with no evidence it matters is not much use.",
  "_answer": "REAL IN-SEASON, NOT USABLE PRE-DRAFT. Supersedes the flat 'no' recorded above.",
  "same_season": {
   "_what": "Spearman(x, season fantasy points), 875 player-seasons, >=20 touches.",
   "RB": {
    "n": 300,
    "share": 0.9619,
    "share_x_neutral": 0.9611,
    "delta": -0.0007
   },
   "WR": {
    "n": 403,
    "share": 0.9189,
    "share_x_neutral": 0.9173,
    "delta": -0.0015
   },
   "TE": {
    "n": 172,
    "share": 0.9134,
    "share_x_neutral": 0.9135,
    "delta": 0.0001
   },
   "_caveat": "Same-season share is an OUTCOME, not a prior \u2014 on draft day you do not know it. This is why the lagged test below is the one that decides anything."
  },
  "lagged": {
   "_what": "Prior-season usage and prior-season team pace predicting the FOLLOWING season's points \u2014 the shape of the actual draft-day question. 588 player-season pairs.",
   "RB": {
    "n": 167,
    "prior_share": 0.686,
    "share_x_neutral": 0.6859,
    "delta": -0.0001,
    "pace_alone": 0.0569
   },
   "WR": {
    "n": 244,
    "prior_share": 0.6496,
    "share_x_neutral": 0.6506,
    "delta": 0.0011,
    "pace_alone": 0.0706
   },
   "TE": {
    "n": 106,
    "prior_share": 0.6201,
    "share_x_neutral": 0.6277,
    "delta": 0.0077,
    "pace_alone": 0.0563
   }
  },
  "why": {
   "_what": "Year-over-year persistence of team pace, all 32 teams.",
   "2023_to_2024": {
    "neutral": 0.194,
    "raw": 0.165
   },
   "2024_to_2025": {
    "neutral": 0.024,
    "raw": -0.065
   },
   "_reading": "A team's pace this year says almost nothing about its pace next year. That is the whole explanation for the null above: a draft-day tiebreak on last season's pace is using a number that will not describe the team being drafted."
  },
  "_what_this_does_NOT_refute": "IN-SEASON use, where you know the current team's current pace and no persistence is required. Nothing measured here speaks against that, and the raw-vs-neutral distinction (rho 0.53 between the two orderings) stands on its own: if pace is ever used, the neutral figure is the one to use.",
  "_which_evidence_is_load_bearing": {
   "_correction_to_my_own_framing": "I led with the rho DELTA (share x pace vs share alone, ~0.001) and that is the WEAKER half. Measured on 2025: team neutral pace spans 42.1 to 61.1, a 1.45x spread, while usage share across players spans 0.024 to 0.303 at p05/p95 \u2014 a 12.7x spread. A multiplier varying by 1.45x cannot move a whole-population rank correlation against a factor varying by 12.7x, so a null delta is partly a statement about the TEST'S POWER and not only about pace.",
   "pace_spread_2025": 1.45,
   "usage_share_spread_p05_p95": 12.7,
   "_what_is_load_bearing": "PERSISTENCE. Last season's team pace correlates with the next season's at rho +0.194 and +0.024. That is what rules pace out as a DRAFT-DAY input, and it does so regardless of whether pace matters at all: a number that does not describe the team being drafted cannot inform the pick. The delta is consistent with that conclusion; it does not carry it.",
   "_what_would_settle_the_remaining_question": "Whether pace matters at all is best asked WITHIN near-ties \u2014 two players at similar usage on differently-paced offences \u2014 where a 1.45x multiplier has room to decide something and a whole-population rho does not. That is a different experiment and it is not run here."
  },
  "within_near_ties": {
   "_what": "The test the whole-population delta had no power to run: residual of season points after removing usage share, correlated with team neutral pace; and the same inside tight usage bands, which is the near-tie question directly.",
   "residual_rho": {
    "RB": 0.1695,
    "WR": 0.1648,
    "TE": 0.2197
   },
   "within_band_mean_rho": {
    "RB": 0.1435,
    "WR": 0.1543,
    "TE": 0.145
   },
   "bands_positive": "17 of 18",
   "_reading": "Pace DOES carry information about production beyond usage share \u2014 consistently, across positions and bands. At least part of that is mechanical rather than a discovery: share x team plays IS absolute touches, so a residual-after-share correlating with pace is close to restating 'volume beats share'. It confirms the module's premise \u2014 a share is a share OF something \u2014 rather than adding to it.",
   "_but": "It does not survive the trip to draft day. Pace persists at rho +0.194 and +0.024 year over year, so last season's figure does not describe the team being drafted. The mechanism is real and the pre-draft OBSERVABILITY is what fails."
  }
 },
